1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,200
It's one of America's favorite pastimes.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:07,480
Its practitioners are tough.

3
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:10,700
And sometimes dangerous.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,440
You gotta go to the hospital later, you go to the hospital.

5
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:19,340
Its popularity has been growing for over a century.

6
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:21,840
But it's not football.

7
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,640
Welcome to the spectacle behind the spectacle.

8
00:00:25,641 --> 00:00:36,540
Join us as we reveal the bizarre rituals, the genteel history, and the over-the-top histrionics of one of America's most inexplicable pastimes.

9
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:40,480
Now, tailgating on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:40,481 --> 00:00:54,481
The frigid pre-dawn hours of a December Chicago morning.

11
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:02,540
Seven men prepare for a grueling odyssey.

12
00:01:02,541 --> 00:01:04,180
Their destination?

13
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:09,620
A frozen expanse of barren tundra, 215 miles north.

14
00:01:09,621 --> 00:01:11,420
Lambeau Field.

15
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:16,180
They bring with them the fervor of the devout football fan.

16
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:19,720
This is my Bears shrine.

17
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,860
Roy Taylor is a 32-year-old technical account manager

18
00:01:24,861 --> 00:01:26,100
from Carroll Stream, Illinois,

19
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:29,320
and a diehard Bear fan since 1979.

20
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,940
A few items of Bears-Packer rivalry here.

21
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:37,300
We, of course, have the shirts that we're not going to walk

22
00:01:37,301 --> 00:01:39,960
into Lambeau Field with unless we're completely crazy.

23
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:42,340
The Green Bay sucks button, of course.

24
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:46,800
And not in complete support of it, but somebody gave me that one.

25
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:50,220
Apparently somebody thinks that Osama wears a cheese head,

26
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:51,480
which we won't argue with.

27
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,980
With 7 1⁄2 hours left before game time,

28
00:01:55,981 --> 00:01:59,200
it's time for these Chicago Bears fans to hit the road.

29
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:01,420
See you tonight.

30
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:05,380
We'll make it back safe as long as we don't get abused too much.

31
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:12,500
Along the way, they see signs of the challenges that lie ahead.

32
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:16,780
That's just to show how tough the Bears really are.

33
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:24,420
Today, they are part of a nationwide pilgrimage of men and women streaming to football stadiums and arenas across the country

34
00:02:24,421 --> 00:02:28,240
for the party that lies just outside in the parking lot.

35
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:31,560
Giants!

36
00:02:31,561 --> 00:02:33,620
We are the best day!

37
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,660
We are the best day!

38
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:42,300
Food, drink, and madness. The movable feast known as tailgating.

39
00:02:42,301 --> 00:02:48,300
What tailgating really is is the new community social.

40
00:02:48,301 --> 00:02:51,371
You know, we've lost the 4th of July party.

41
00:02:51,372 --> 00:02:55,300
We've lost the ability to socialize with our neighbors.

42
00:02:55,301 --> 00:02:59,300
Tailgating really is the revving up period for the fans.

43
00:02:59,301 --> 00:03:04,300
It's when they get charged up and ready to go and cheer on their gridiron warriors.

44
00:03:04,301 --> 00:03:06,980
E-A-G-L-E-S-E-O!

45
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:10,700
People, they cook out and they bring a keg of beer,

46
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,320
and it starts at 7 in the morning, and the game usually starts about 2,

47
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:18,260
and, you know, it's a crazy, crazy time.

48
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:20,300
Tailgetting in general is just about people.

49
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:22,780
It's the people you want to be with, it's the people you love,

50
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:26,020
it's just being together 6, 7, 8 times a year.

51
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:26,940
That's what it's all about.

52
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:31,520
It's really about togetherness more, I think, than it is totally the love of the sport.

53
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:33,220
It's awesome, it's amazing,

54
00:03:33,221 --> 00:03:35,440
It brings up the hype, brings up the spirit.

55
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:37,660
It gets everybody psyched for the game.

56
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:43,740
The players train hard to perform, and the fans, they train, so to speak, prior to going in for the game.

57
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:46,960
Each week, millions of people hit the gates all over America.

58
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:53,040
Whether it's State College Pennsylvania, Miami's Orange Bowl, or the Plains of Texas,

59
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,380
the true fans are there week in, week out.

60
00:03:57,080 --> 00:04:00,780
You've got to wonder how this all began and what kept it going.

61
00:04:00,781 --> 00:04:05,660
The origins of what we now call tailgating are pretty murky.

62
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:11,960
What's clear is that through the years, any big spectacle, whether it be sport or battle, brought community together.

63
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:19,840
As depicted in this New York Herald illustration, many have called modern-day football players the Roman warriors of their time,

64
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:24,400
making those outside the Coliseum on game day the first tailgaters.

65
00:04:24,401 --> 00:04:32,140
It's very likely that tailgating was a major feature or component of the Roman gladiators.

66
00:04:35,260 --> 00:04:38,340
It's true that while the gladiators battled to the death,

67
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:42,200
outside the Colosseum, Romans were enjoying meals of grilled fish.

68
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:46,200
Not exactly tailgating in the modern sense, but it is a start.

69
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:54,120
Others suggest tailgating started 2,000 years later during the Civil War.

70
00:04:54,400 --> 00:05:02,060
In 1861, spectators lined up in the Virginia town of Manassas to watch the action of what would eventually be called the Battle of Bull Run.

71
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:09,740
According to Captain John Tidball, a Union commander, all manner of people were represented in this crowd,

72
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:16,480
from most brave and noble senators to the few women who had driven out in carts loaded with pies and other edibles.

73
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:23,580
Spectators were directing their carriages towards the sound of war to get closer to the action and hopefully see the battle firsthand.

74
00:05:23,581 --> 00:05:27,580
Unfortunately, they got too close, and when Union soldiers hastily retreated,

75
00:05:27,581 --> 00:05:32,580
the spectators and soldiers collided with each other in the confusion.

76
00:05:32,581 --> 00:05:36,580
But true tailgating would not evolve for another 50 years.

77
00:05:36,581 --> 00:05:40,580
It was then that a sport with breeding, class, and sophistication

78
00:05:40,581 --> 00:05:43,580
would make this phenomena a reality.

79
00:05:43,581 --> 00:05:48,580
Football was sport, but it was also tied with colleges,

80
00:05:48,581 --> 00:05:52,580
and it was tied with more breeding and sort of an upper-class atmosphere.

81
00:05:52,581 --> 00:05:57,840
And I think that's one of the reasons why Ivy League football got so popular early.

82
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:01,220
Since it was centered at the elite universities,

83
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:04,820
the New York Times considered early football a gentleman's game.

84
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:07,960
Newspapers such as The World and other media

85
00:06:07,961 --> 00:06:12,100
seemed to show that the crowds were more interested in the social scene than the game itself.

86
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:15,560
As for the pregame festivities,

87
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:18,560
nothing beat a ride in a horse-drawn carriage

88
00:06:18,561 --> 00:06:22,100
with well-heeled collegiates roaring down the avenue on the way to the game.

89
00:06:22,101 --> 00:06:28,520
Ladies clutching their muffs, the gents waving their hats, as illustrated on this cover of the Saturday Evening Post.

90
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:34,880
High society tailgaters running around in elaborate carriages?

91
00:06:34,881 --> 00:06:42,220
How does that compare to the gaudy rigs, sloppy foods, and beer-guzzling behavior that signifies modern tailgating?

92
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:46,080
Today's tailgating diehard is anything but genteel.

93
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:50,900
Just check out the windswept wilds of Lambeau Field, home to the Green Bay Packers.

94
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:58,100
Lambeau has been in use for longer than any other stadium in the NFL since 1957.

95
00:06:58,101 --> 00:07:03,560
It ain't pretty, but it's hallowed football ground from the field to the parking lot.

96
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:08,480
Here, some of tailgating's hardiest toughen out for every Frigid home game.

97
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:13,940
It doesn't matter if it's 20 below, 30 below.

98
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:16,260
We get here, we do the same thing.

99
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:17,620
We just dress a little warmer.

100
00:07:17,621 --> 00:07:23,340
Green Bay fans are great because living in Green Bay is a tough life right there.

101
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:25,880
But they've got the Green Bay Packers there.

102
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:28,460
They've got a professional football team who does pretty darn well.

103
00:07:28,980 --> 00:07:30,960
So their life revolves around that.

104
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:35,900
Since 1921, the Packers have owned this small Midwestern town.

105
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:38,820
Yeah, baby! Green Bay!

106
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:42,380
I think we put anybody else to shame.

107
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,220
And I know there's other teams that will say that,

108
00:07:44,221 --> 00:07:47,500
but if you haven't tailgated Lambeau, you haven't tailgated.

109
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:49,700
Lambeau is the best. There's nothing like Lambeau.

110
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:54,420
I've been going to Packer games for probably 20 years, 22 years,

111
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:58,640
and we tailgate every game, and we've been to away games,

112
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,480
and there's nothing like Packer fans.

113
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:02,840
They're obnoxious, and it's awesome.

114
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:03,960
They're just a lot of fun.

115
00:08:04,060 --> 00:08:04,760
I agree.

116
00:08:04,761 --> 00:08:05,461
Woo!

117
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:06,620
Woo!

118
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:11,740
Do it, baby!

119
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:15,660
We've had about 70 years of practice, and we've got it down perfectly.

120
00:08:15,661 --> 00:08:19,413
Hi, I am the Pacalope. This is a great day for football.

121
00:08:19,414 --> 00:08:23,300
The Packers, the Bears, on a frozen tundra, Lambeau Field.

122
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:34,200
In 1999, the Pacalope, otherwise known as Larry Primo, was the first Packers fan to be inducted into the Pro Football Hall of Fame in Canton, Ohio.

123
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:37,860
There's a story behind the nickname of this lifelong Packer backer.

124
00:08:37,861 --> 00:08:42,260
When the Packers ran away to Super Bowl XXXI, I figured championship horns, championship team.

125
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:47,360
So I ended up mounting a 10-point rack onto the same vintage old Packer helmet.

126
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:51,980
I added some Rardi Gras beads and a cheese wedge, and I became the Pack-A-Lope.

127
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:57,400
Like most dyed-in-the-wool tailgaters, the Pack-A-Lope has spent a lot of time in the parking lot.

128
00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:02,400
In the parking lot, there's an element of personal expression for some people.

129
00:09:02,401 --> 00:09:08,220
Some fans enjoy putting hats on with antlers or horns.

130
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:10,500
Here we go.

131
00:09:11,300 --> 00:09:14,480
But you don't have to be a Packer fan to be a true blue tailgater.

132
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:19,560
People like Tailgate Russ in Philly, the Penn State Triumvirate at State College,

133
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:25,340
and Dolphin Dad of Miami exude passion for extreme tailgating all over the country.

134
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:26,880
This is football.

135
00:09:26,881 --> 00:09:37,860
I think the hardcore fan identifies with the team and sees him or herself as a part of the team and indeed a contributor to the team.

136
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,880
Being a football fan for many men and women is very much a part of their identity, who they are.

137
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:52,320
It's a way that they get some value out of life that they're not going to get during their regular work week.

138
00:09:52,321 --> 00:09:58,220
The question we have to ask, then, is how did football tailgating come quite so far?

139
00:09:58,460 --> 00:10:02,300
From the old days, when elegantly clad collegiates cheered on their team,

140
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:06,080
to today, when the tailgating party is out of control.

141
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,640
The rules are, you've got to let it all hang out.

142
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:13,340
When we come back, we'll tackle a few tailgating traditions, both old and new.

143
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:18,340
Despite the long distances traveled by some diehard tailgaters,

144
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:21,780
50% travel less than one hour to the stadium.

145
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:25,080
17% travel between two and four hours,

146
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:29,160
and just 8% travel over four hours to reach the game.

147
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:31,620
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

148
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:33,760
Oh!

149
00:10:35,980 --> 00:10:38,100
C-A-N-E-S-K!

150
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:41,160
Game day at the Orange Bowl in Miami, Florida,

151
00:10:41,161 --> 00:10:43,580
where the Hurricanes are going to take on Virginia Tech.

152
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:46,500
Outside the stadium, the weather is balmy,

153
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:50,480
the men are beery, and the women, let's call them scantily clack.

154
00:10:50,481 --> 00:10:52,880
The atmosphere is intense.

155
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:55,940
The Orange Bowl party is one of the wildest on the East Coast.

156
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:05,540
The screaming, the partying, the intensity, all are hallmarks of a big league tailgate.

157
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:10,540
Here, emotions run high, sometimes with a little help.

158
00:11:12,820 --> 00:11:17,300
Captain Morgan Spice Rum and Hurricane Football, two winning combinations.

159
00:11:17,301 --> 00:11:21,720
Sure, some of this passion is liquid in nature.

160
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:23,520
I think you should have some.

161
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:24,420
There you go.

162
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:30,740
We all know the act of tailgating is usually marked by a creative approach to the consumption of alcohol.

163
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:32,260
A-E-S-T!

164
00:11:32,600 --> 00:11:33,300
Shot!

165
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,220
First roll tailgating, no water.

166
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:36,300
Beer!

167
00:11:36,301 --> 00:11:37,300
More beer!

168
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:39,700
Bring your boda with your hunter-proof schnapps.

169
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:41,460
It's not really about the food.

170
00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:45,180
It's about sneaking beer into the game and drinking beer in the parking lot.

171
00:11:45,181 --> 00:11:48,940
So if you get caught, sneak a beer in the game, you're still thankfully drunk.

172
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:49,900
Rule number six.

173
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:52,520
You go back to rule number one where it gets more drinking.

174
00:11:54,180 --> 00:11:59,000
But there's something more to a college tailgate, and it's not just all the booze.

175
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:02,600
There are so many personal feelings that are associated with a college campus.

176
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:08,800
People mature on a college campus, and they really become who they become there.

177
00:12:08,801 --> 00:12:11,360
So that's what makes college football special,

178
00:12:11,361 --> 00:12:16,460
and that's really what sets it apart from the pro tailgating atmosphere.

179
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:24,560
So was college tailgating much different at its start?

180
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:25,820
You decide.

181
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:40,120
College tailgating started at the early football games of Princeton, Harvard, Yale, and Rutgers

182
00:12:40,121 --> 00:12:43,720
with the excitement soon spreading through papers such as the world.

183
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:48,360
At the very first football game on Saturday, November 6, 1869,

184
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:51,520
the Princeton team was greeted by their Rutgers rivals

185
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:53,920
at the train station in New Brunswick, New Jersey.

186
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:56,360
It was all very civilized.

187
00:12:56,980 --> 00:12:58,680
The game wasn't going to be played until the afternoon,

188
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:02,760
so the Rutgers students took the Princeton students to a billiard parlor,

189
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:06,240
took them around to show them the sights around campus,

190
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:08,760
and made kind of a social event out of it

191
00:13:08,761 --> 00:13:12,120
until it was time to strip off their coats and roll up their sleeves and play.

192
00:13:12,420 --> 00:13:16,760
It wasn't long before the impromptu football social event grew in popularity,

193
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:20,540
becoming front-page news in papers such as The World.

194
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:22,920
The games were played in the early afternoon normally,

195
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:26,200
and they were always accompanied by a feast.

196
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:29,260
And the game always brought people together,

197
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:31,880
and it made for a day-long event of food and fun.

198
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:36,120
In 1876, the Intercollegiate Football Association

199
00:13:36,121 --> 00:13:39,640
decided that the best two teams would play each other on Thanksgiving Day.

200
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:43,520
They defectively created the first national championship game

201
00:13:43,521 --> 00:13:48,000
and, more importantly, one of the biggest tailgating events of the year.

202
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:52,800
They picked Thanksgiving in part because it was a holiday already.

203
00:13:52,960 --> 00:13:57,340
People would be out of school, and it was right after the end of what would normally be the football season.

204
00:13:57,840 --> 00:14:03,520
At the 1893 Thanksgiving Day game, over 50,000 spectators brought their lunches,

205
00:14:03,521 --> 00:14:06,940
tailgating at their carriages for the Yale-Princeton matchup.

206
00:14:07,180 --> 00:14:09,800
Everybody in New York would come out to the games.

207
00:14:09,801 --> 00:14:16,040
The whole week before the game, you'd have shopkeepers hanging up the colors of Yale and Princeton in their windows.

208
00:14:16,620 --> 00:14:18,940
You'd have flags draped outside of the hotels.

209
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:21,200
Newspapers would be giving coverage.

210
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:24,020
So everybody got into the act.

211
00:14:24,220 --> 00:14:25,100
And at the fields?

212
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:28,960
Well, activities there were really starting to look like tailgating,

213
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:31,580
at least as far as one critical ingredient.

214
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:38,340
Alcohol was very important, I think more important in people's lives generally than it is today.

215
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:44,140
You read game accounts of games being played back in the 1880s and certainly into the 1890s.

216
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:50,040
And there are stories of Yale students throwing empty liquor bottles at policemen on the field

217
00:14:50,041 --> 00:14:54,020
if they didn't like the way the policemen were pushing them back up into the stands.

218
00:14:55,980 --> 00:15:00,360
Rowdy behavior and the times brought about the next big development in tailgating.

219
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:03,900
Colleges began to build massive stadiums on campus

220
00:15:03,901 --> 00:15:07,760
to keep an eye on crowds that were getting bigger and more raucous with every year.

221
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:12,920
By the end of World War I, memorial stadiums were being built to honor fallen soldiers.

222
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:18,840
With larger stadiums and smaller ticket prices, the arena doors opened to the common man.

223
00:15:19,320 --> 00:15:22,800
By the 1920s, tailgaters only needed one more thing.

224
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:26,280
Tailgates. Yeah, the final piece of the puzzle.

225
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:28,760
The good old American station wagon.

226
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:34,940
In the early 1900s, the station wagon wasn't for mom to haul groceries around.

227
00:15:35,280 --> 00:15:41,340
Originally called Depot Hacks, they were developed to help transport luggage and passengers from the train station.

228
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:46,160
It wasn't until the 1920s that the first factory-produced station wagon was introduced.

229
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:52,480
In 1929, Ford came out with a Model A station wagon featuring a foldable tailgate.

230
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:56,360
Soon, automobile owners were doing more than hauling luggage.

231
00:15:56,361 --> 00:16:04,760
They would get together and open up the back of the car, get there early, enjoy alcohol.

232
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:10,180
If they had it, they'd have food, they'd have cakes, all sorts of things.

233
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:14,360
Really make a pre-game lunch out of it and have their friends together.

234
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:17,800
A lot of the same sort of things you would see today, just the model of the cars would be different.

235
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,060
Over the years, the station wagon evolved into a sleek all-metal vehicle,

236
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:24,540
and the tailgate would get a makeover, too.

237
00:16:25,180 --> 00:16:28,840
A new kind of tailgate was developed that opened from the side or the bottom.

238
00:16:29,580 --> 00:16:33,300
Station wagons ruled when it came to tailgating in the stadium lots.

239
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:40,360
More than 35 years later, it's still the same.

240
00:16:40,740 --> 00:16:42,820
Just ask the guys outside the Orange Bowl.

241
00:16:43,380 --> 00:16:49,540
Over the years, tailgating has grown from a few devoted fans to a mass party with an almost unlimited guest list.

242
00:16:50,540 --> 00:16:53,140
Our tailgate has taken on a life of its own.

243
00:16:53,141 --> 00:16:57,900
The originals invited relatives and friends, and they've invited relatives and friends.

244
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:02,220
It's kind of neat that people I played with and players I coached, they all sort of know where we are.

245
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:07,020
And we have guys from Byron Blades and Ted Hendricks coming back, and guys I played with, Gary Dunn,

246
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:08,683
and guys will travel and we played in.

247
00:17:08,684 --> 00:17:10,640
They live in Tampa, different parts of Florida.

248
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:14,500
And they'll all come in, and we have kind of that mini, our own mini reunion.

249
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:16,060
Let's go, Keith!

250
00:17:16,540 --> 00:17:22,960
Being a college football fan, for many men and women, is very much a part of their identity.

251
00:17:23,140 --> 00:17:23,840
who they are.

252
00:17:23,841 --> 00:17:27,140
Four or five days out of the week, you might catch them in their team's colors.

253
00:17:27,600 --> 00:17:32,380
It's something that people not only identify them with, but they identify themselves with.

254
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:37,680
It's very much a part of who they are. So we've got the game. We've got the fans.

255
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,832
We've got the parking lot. And we've finally got the tailgates.

256
00:17:41,833 --> 00:17:43,900
Now, let's get this party started.

257
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:51,220
Tailgaters are serious about their sport. 46% tailgates six to ten times a season.

258
00:17:51,221 --> 00:18:00,220
25% tailgate 11 to 15 times, and a dedicated 8% tailgate at 16 games or more per season.

259
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,220
Tailgating will return on Modern Marvels.

260
00:18:05,340 --> 00:18:07,880
Where would tailgating be without food?

261
00:18:08,060 --> 00:18:10,840
We got the best wings on the road right here. Oh, man.

262
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:17,660
Whether grilled, smoked, baked, or fried, it's the chow that really matters in the stadium lot.

263
00:18:17,661 --> 00:18:21,760
You can walk unannounced into almost anyone's tailgating party.

264
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:27,840
You can have a completely empty stomach when you walk up, but by the time you leave, you will be completely full.

265
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:29,540
We have about five fried turkeys coming.

266
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:32,640
It's a free dinner. Just go around and stuff your face.

267
00:18:33,140 --> 00:18:38,000
But what is it about football that makes stuffing your face full of meat sound so good?

268
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:45,600
Sure, it tastes great, fuels the body's engine, but to diehard tailgaters, cooking a meal isn't about sustenance.

269
00:18:45,601 --> 00:18:46,900
Oh, no.

270
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:53,120
It's about rivalry, intimidation, and, of course, winning.

271
00:18:54,380 --> 00:18:56,760
Get big! Get! Get! Get! Get!

272
00:18:57,140 --> 00:18:58,240
Look at this movie!

273
00:18:58,680 --> 00:19:02,300
Tailgating is about winning a game, and it's about getting yourself ready for the game.

274
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:03,300
Is that right, Sebastian?

275
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:09,480
Territoriality is a major part of the tailgating phenomenon.

276
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:14,820
It might be, you know, who's got the best cooker or the best grill?

277
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:17,300
Absolutely, that stuff goes on.

278
00:19:17,301 --> 00:19:21,560
They're part of the war. They're part of the competition.

279
00:19:22,340 --> 00:19:23,760
And they enjoy it.

280
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:30,580
A case in point, the early football rivals Princeton and Yale

281
00:19:30,581 --> 00:19:32,600
back at the turn of the 20th century.

282
00:19:33,380 --> 00:19:35,240
Princeton fans bought and ate oranges.

283
00:19:35,820 --> 00:19:37,940
Not a particularly robust meal, perhaps,

284
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:40,120
but a food that matched their school's color.

285
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:41,700
The Yale spectators?

286
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:46,360
Well, they did what any fan would do in the face of a fruit-wielding adversary.

287
00:19:46,361 --> 00:19:55,260
The Yale fans dyed them blue, and if you had the stomach to buy a blue-colored orange and you were a Yale fan, that's probably what you would have eaten as well.

288
00:19:56,280 --> 00:20:02,760
Before long, collegiate fans began preparing elaborate picnic suppers to be eaten on the tops of carriages at competitions.

289
00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:11,160
There they could root for their comrades in arms, eat some beefsteak, potatoes, and chops, and enjoy a good bit of armchair quarterbacking, too.

290
00:20:11,161 --> 00:20:19,260
It was customary at the time for fans of both teams to engage in a wild game and fish supper before the game.

291
00:20:19,660 --> 00:20:22,660
They'd watch the game and then after they'd come back and eat the leftovers

292
00:20:22,661 --> 00:20:26,900
and talk about the daring exploits of the young men on the field.

293
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:34,100
Today, tailgating is still all about fueling up for the big game, savoring the competition,

294
00:20:35,100 --> 00:20:36,620
people coming together,

295
00:20:36,621 --> 00:20:41,140
and eating bear meat.

296
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:43,160
Yep, bear meat.

297
00:20:43,161 --> 00:20:45,160
I'm not saying, or maybe.

298
00:20:45,161 --> 00:20:48,160
We killed two black bears with bow and arrow archery.

299
00:20:48,161 --> 00:20:50,160
And bear still.

300
00:20:50,161 --> 00:20:52,160
Matter of fact, we have a spare bear.

301
00:20:52,161 --> 00:20:59,160
Obviously, things have changed just a bit since fans first chowed down 100 years ago.

302
00:20:59,161 --> 00:21:06,160
For these tailgaters at Lambeau Field, the food is the game, and the game is food.

303
00:21:06,161 --> 00:21:10,160
Why settle for just booing the competition when you can eat it?

304
00:21:10,161 --> 00:21:13,160
We had a great time, 300 people plus.

305
00:21:13,161 --> 00:21:17,160
Just a great place to be on a Packer Bear game.

306
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:21,160
Giants are number one!

307
00:21:21,161 --> 00:21:24,160
And Green Bay isn't the only place where tailgating fans

308
00:21:24,161 --> 00:21:27,160
ritually sacrifice their opponents on a grill.

309
00:21:27,161 --> 00:21:30,160
Worse yet are the callous few, namely Giants fans,

310
00:21:30,161 --> 00:21:34,160
whose rivalry with the Philadelphia Eagles spurs them to barbecue

311
00:21:34,161 --> 00:21:36,160
what appears to be an endangered species.

312
00:21:36,161 --> 00:21:38,160
Breasted Philadelphia Eagle right here.

313
00:21:38,161 --> 00:21:41,120
It's a rare breed, but we cook them up good here at Giant Stadium.

314
00:21:41,480 --> 00:21:43,180
Don't worry, it's actually chicken.

315
00:21:43,660 --> 00:21:45,000
But you get the idea.

316
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:49,500
New York Jet fans make their loyalties known by grilling up dolphin sandwiches.

317
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:52,720
No, not the mammal, the fish.

318
00:21:52,920 --> 00:21:53,900
Dolphins are his favorite.

319
00:21:54,460 --> 00:21:55,540
He loves to eat dolphins.

320
00:21:58,500 --> 00:22:01,860
But tailgating menus aren't always about eating mascots.

321
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:06,320
At football stadiums from New York to Michigan, Pennsylvania to Louisiana,

322
00:22:06,321 --> 00:22:11,000
some surprisingly inventive chefs base their carte du jour on their region.

323
00:22:12,620 --> 00:22:15,000
South Philly, how can you be without calamari?

324
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:18,740
There's no smell-o-vision, but this is smell-o-vision, okay?

325
00:22:19,360 --> 00:22:20,060
Yeah, man!

326
00:22:21,100 --> 00:22:22,920
Oh, so damn beautiful!

327
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:25,300
The kind of food you see runs the gamut.

328
00:22:25,440 --> 00:22:28,620
I mean, you see everything from people doing big pots of jambalaya

329
00:22:28,621 --> 00:22:32,320
to just picking up some fried chicken and some potato chips at the grocery store.

330
00:22:32,321 --> 00:22:39,320
Down in the bayou, tailgaters at Louisiana State show their home pride by cooking it up with a Cajun flair.

331
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:47,320
From the stone oven roasted pig to Creole favorites like chambalaya, gumbo, and lobster bisque.

332
00:22:48,380 --> 00:22:54,360
You come down here and you're going to eat food that's as good or better as a five-star restaurant up in the north.

333
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:59,600
It's become a tradition and it becomes something that you spend your Saturday afternoons in the fall doing.

334
00:22:59,601 --> 00:23:03,540
LSU! LSU! LSU!

335
00:23:03,740 --> 00:23:08,100
In Texas, we're rich people.

336
00:23:08,540 --> 00:23:10,420
And this is the way we do it. We're fine.

337
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:11,760
Is this good food?

338
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:13,320
It's very good food.

339
00:23:14,500 --> 00:23:18,580
443 miles northwest of LSU, in the parking lot of Texas Stadium,

340
00:23:19,100 --> 00:23:23,720
Cowboy fans have been gathering for over 37 years every Thanksgiving day.

341
00:23:24,340 --> 00:23:28,940
What it is, like a bunch of really cool friends that you love get together.

342
00:23:28,941 --> 00:23:32,380
Win, lose, or draw. Thanksgiving game is always the best.

343
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:33,380
A lot of good food.

344
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:38,020
There, they blitz their birds southern style, deep fried and greasy.

345
00:23:38,260 --> 00:23:40,000
Now this is living large right here.

346
00:23:40,360 --> 00:23:42,340
It's the moistest turkey you'll ever put in your mouth.

347
00:23:43,100 --> 00:23:46,920
Uh-huh, yeah, eating fried turkey in Dallas is all very interesting,

348
00:23:47,260 --> 00:23:51,500
but what's tailgating in Texas without a piece of cheerleading?

349
00:23:51,740 --> 00:23:53,400
This is our tailgate party right here.

350
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:57,020
We get to eat our meal and have a lot of fun and talk and get ready for the game.

351
00:23:57,021 --> 00:24:02,940
A lot of people think us Texas girls are petite little things, but we like to chow down on turkey legs like everyone else.

352
00:24:04,180 --> 00:24:05,380
Go Cowboys.

353
00:24:06,040 --> 00:24:06,740
Yeah.

354
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:12,120
All fans take pride in their parking lot grunt.

355
00:24:12,460 --> 00:24:14,360
It's their duty to deliver the goods.

356
00:24:15,140 --> 00:24:20,420
Tailgaters at Penn State are no slouches when it comes to serving inspiring feasts in the parking lot.

357
00:24:20,421 --> 00:24:22,660
football Saturday at Penn State,

358
00:24:23,140 --> 00:24:24,680
that Happy Valley turns into

359
00:24:24,681 --> 00:24:26,220
a major media center,

360
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:28,420
a mecca for people. It's probably

361
00:24:28,421 --> 00:24:30,560
maybe one of the largest cities in

362
00:24:30,561 --> 00:24:32,940
Pennsylvania or in that area during that time.

363
00:24:33,140 --> 00:24:34,920
Every Saturday here when Penn State's

364
00:24:34,921 --> 00:24:36,700
home, you'll have people lined up to park

365
00:24:36,701 --> 00:24:38,680
in these parking lots. We're here

366
00:24:38,681 --> 00:24:40,280
at 6 o'clock in the morning waiting for an 8 o'clock

367
00:24:40,281 --> 00:24:41,720
parking spot to get here.

368
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:44,660
Our tailgate alone will probably have 150 people.

369
00:24:44,980 --> 00:24:45,900
Fight our state!

370
00:24:46,020 --> 00:24:47,540
Fight our state!

371
00:24:47,541 --> 00:24:48,241
TOTAL AND I

372
00:24:48,242 --> 00:24:50,800
TRIP YOUR GAME AND WIN!

373
00:24:50,801 --> 00:24:51,800
Yeah!

374
00:24:51,801 --> 00:24:53,580
Tried to get a nice cold beer.

375
00:24:53,581 --> 00:24:55,660
What you do is you come over here and check out the Good Grub.

376
00:24:55,661 --> 00:24:57,260
And here's one of our staple attractions.

377
00:24:57,261 --> 00:24:59,340
So damn good at 9 o'clock at 1.

378
00:24:59,341 --> 00:25:01,240
Mmm.

379
00:25:01,241 --> 00:25:03,620
Oh, man.

380
00:25:03,621 --> 00:25:05,760
Over here, we've got the best barbecue, beef barbecue,

381
00:25:05,761 --> 00:25:06,860
right here cooking.

382
00:25:06,861 --> 00:25:08,420
We've got about 20 pounds of beef.

383
00:25:08,421 --> 00:25:09,320
20 pounds of barbecue.

384
00:25:09,321 --> 00:25:10,520
20 pounds of barbecue today.

385
00:25:10,521 --> 00:25:11,720
Easily we'll go through that.

386
00:25:11,721 --> 00:25:13,380
We've got dressing on the inside.

387
00:25:13,381 --> 00:25:15,640
We've got some gravy warming up right here.

388
00:25:15,641 --> 00:25:17,240
We've got some Manhattan clam chowder.

389
00:25:17,241 --> 00:25:18,720
Not the McClamb, not the McClamb.

390
00:25:19,060 --> 00:25:23,640
And a little bit later after the game, we had some bratwurst from Milwaukee that we were going to cook up.

391
00:25:23,820 --> 00:25:26,640
But remember this, the next time you're at a Penn State game,

392
00:25:26,820 --> 00:25:32,200
if they can't buy the meat in the local supermarket, Nittany Lion fans will hunt it down themselves.

393
00:25:32,460 --> 00:25:33,580
A little venison roast.

394
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:38,040
He actually ran this thing down and killed this deer with his bare hand.

395
00:25:38,041 --> 00:25:40,360
This guy right here, he's the one.

396
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:44,380
It fought, you know, but choked it out, skinned it barehanded.

397
00:25:44,720 --> 00:25:46,520
We are Penn State!

398
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:47,300
We are!

399
00:25:47,320 --> 00:25:48,060
Penn State!

400
00:25:48,140 --> 00:25:48,840
We are!

401
00:25:48,860 --> 00:25:49,560
Penn State!

402
00:25:49,620 --> 00:25:50,320
We are!

403
00:25:51,740 --> 00:25:58,540
Well, as you can see, tailgating fare goes way beyond hamburgers and hot dogs,

404
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:00,520
and so do a few other items.

405
00:26:00,600 --> 00:26:03,480
People in Oakland, they show up in their Coronas and their Subarus.

406
00:26:03,481 --> 00:26:09,400
We show up in our $400,000 Silver Eagle bus.

407
00:26:10,440 --> 00:26:13,580
At a loss for what to make for your next tailgate party?

408
00:26:13,581 --> 00:26:23,720
Log on to the commissioner's website, tailgating.com, for recipes for everything from Pro Bowl kielbasa to Colt's fruit pizza to Nittany lion spread.

409
00:26:24,580 --> 00:26:27,260
Tailgating will return on Modern Marvels.

410
00:26:29,040 --> 00:26:29,763
Tailgating.

411
00:26:29,764 --> 00:26:36,660
It started as a simple barbecue in the back of an old wagon and has exploded into a money-making bonanza.

412
00:26:37,380 --> 00:26:40,260
We've got generators. We've got a 2,700-watt sound system.

413
00:26:40,460 --> 00:26:42,380
The most essential item now is the van.

414
00:26:42,381 --> 00:26:44,420
135 grand, you can own this thing.

415
00:26:44,700 --> 00:26:46,740
He brings his own stereo system here.

416
00:26:46,840 --> 00:26:48,140
We've got fans, we've got air conditioning.

417
00:26:48,420 --> 00:26:51,620
We have a 1,000-watt generator that runs the stereo inside the van.

418
00:26:51,700 --> 00:26:53,880
We have our DirecTV satellite this year.

419
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:55,460
We bought parts on the Internet.

420
00:26:55,780 --> 00:26:58,520
Welcome to the big business of tailgating.

421
00:27:01,940 --> 00:27:06,180
NFL football is one of the most popular spectator sports in the United States.

422
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:09,300
Of the over 20 million fans attending games each year,

423
00:27:09,301 --> 00:27:11,920
it's estimated that a quarter of them are tailgaters.

424
00:27:12,380 --> 00:27:17,040
and that these fans could spend over $100 million a year on food and gear.

425
00:27:19,160 --> 00:27:23,300
The practice of tailgating or this cultural phenomenon of tailgating

426
00:27:23,301 --> 00:27:28,820
has really spurred a whole wave of spending on items just to help you tailgate better

427
00:27:28,821 --> 00:27:31,060
and to be the best tailgater you can be.

428
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:34,760
It's a little bit of a competition of a game within a game.

429
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:37,960
What's the person in the next space bringing this year?

430
00:27:37,961 --> 00:27:42,600
When did tailgating become such a franchise?

431
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:50,120
Beginning in the 1960s, manufacturers began to realize that tailgating had become a popular ritual in the college football season.

432
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:55,260
Along with the aroma of grilled burgers and bratwurst, the smell of money was in the air.

433
00:27:55,840 --> 00:28:00,120
Clothing outfitters like Abercrombie & Fitch advertised in the New York Times,

434
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:03,720
products specifically targeted to the jet-set Ivy League crowd.

435
00:28:03,721 --> 00:28:06,940
Their motto, all you need is the station wagon.

436
00:28:07,460 --> 00:28:09,480
Abercrombies can supply all the rest.

437
00:28:09,820 --> 00:28:12,020
How about a horse blanket tweed sport coat for $95?

438
00:28:12,540 --> 00:28:17,340
Or an English wicker vagabond kit with thermos bottles and a nifty stainless steel box for only $30?

439
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:20,360
Oh, and don't forget that silk-knit English tie.

440
00:28:20,600 --> 00:28:21,940
A steal at only $6.

441
00:28:25,220 --> 00:28:29,560
But the days of gentle and refined tailgating were about to get sacked.

442
00:28:30,080 --> 00:28:33,660
Like the rest of America in the 1960s, football was changing.

443
00:28:33,820 --> 00:28:35,740
No longer a sport of the elite.

444
00:28:35,741 --> 00:28:38,780
it was louder, rowdier, and made for TV.

445
00:28:39,320 --> 00:28:43,000
And it all happened on January 15, 1967.

446
00:28:44,140 --> 00:28:46,840
That's right, Super Bowl numero uno.

447
00:28:49,080 --> 00:28:52,060
Soon, players like Joe Namath became celebrities

448
00:28:52,061 --> 00:28:56,120
as the game of football transformed into the new national pastime.

449
00:28:56,760 --> 00:28:58,480
And it only got bigger.

450
00:28:58,940 --> 00:29:03,020
In 1970, Monday Night Football was launched into primetime television,

451
00:29:03,021 --> 00:29:06,520
bringing over 33 million American viewers with it.

452
00:29:07,120 --> 00:29:12,320
In 1996, Super Bowl XXX became the most-watched televised event in history.

453
00:29:13,540 --> 00:29:17,460
All this popularity spelled one thing, moolah.

454
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:19,820
Football wasn't just a sport anymore.

455
00:29:20,120 --> 00:29:22,360
It was a business, a big business.

456
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:26,200
And today, football big business knows no bounds.

457
00:29:26,780 --> 00:29:30,320
Stadiums are full of sponsors, advertising, and merchandising.

458
00:29:30,321 --> 00:29:31,580
We've got one for the cameraman.

459
00:29:31,581 --> 00:29:34,200
And a whole lot of tailgating gear.

460
00:29:35,020 --> 00:29:38,480
It's an enormous opportunity for marketing. There's no question about that.

461
00:29:39,500 --> 00:29:42,300
In salesman's lingo, there's a name for this.

462
00:29:42,620 --> 00:29:44,680
It's called a big fat niche.

463
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:47,780
And oh man, how it's being filled.

464
00:29:47,960 --> 00:29:49,480
That's what I'm talking about.

465
00:29:50,780 --> 00:29:58,880
One of the byproducts of the growth and the popularity of sports and tailgating is all the things that are associated with tailgating.

466
00:29:58,881 --> 00:30:04,240
Tailgating gear over the years has gotten more complex, has gotten more expensive.

467
00:30:04,940 --> 00:30:09,320
Where it used to be you just brought out a grill with some charcoal,

468
00:30:10,060 --> 00:30:13,320
now you're bringing out probably your whole backyard.

469
00:30:13,780 --> 00:30:18,320
There is a tremendous amount of stuff that you can buy to go out and do this.

470
00:30:19,440 --> 00:30:20,540
Tremendous is right.

471
00:30:20,980 --> 00:30:24,160
These days, if you plan on tailgating, you better get out your wallet

472
00:30:24,161 --> 00:30:27,680
because there's plenty of stuff you need in order to pull it off right.

473
00:30:27,681 --> 00:30:30,620
We've got the big red, I call it the circus tent.

474
00:30:30,920 --> 00:30:31,940
We've got the picnic table.

475
00:30:32,040 --> 00:30:32,760
We've got the grill.

476
00:30:33,180 --> 00:30:34,120
We've got the coolers.

477
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:36,780
You've got your rubber-made containers, right, for all your paper products.

478
00:30:36,820 --> 00:30:39,380
I've got my Finsulate gloves, Gore-Tex gloves.

479
00:30:39,680 --> 00:30:41,300
I've got my Cabela's boots on.

480
00:30:41,420 --> 00:30:42,320
We've got the generator.

481
00:30:42,420 --> 00:30:43,620
That's an essential every week.

482
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:49,580
These guys at Giant Stadium spent $259 on this thing, the Daiquiri Whacker.

483
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:54,180
It's a two-horsepower gas-fired weed trimmer combined with a blender.

484
00:30:54,181 --> 00:31:04,787
There are coolers with built-in stereos, easy-up tents, portable toilets, flagpoles, picnic tables, flasks, seat cushions for your tailgate, and don't forget the

485
00:31:04,788 --> 00:31:05,488
football.

486
00:31:05,860 --> 00:31:08,100
We have our cowboy grill, our cowboy table and chairs.

487
00:31:08,260 --> 00:31:10,940
You've got to have a tent. If you don't have a tent, forget about it. Get out.

488
00:31:11,020 --> 00:31:13,140
It's called an endless summer, and it's just perfect.

489
00:31:13,141 --> 00:31:16,940
Put it up on the rack and set it up, hook it up to the propane, and it keeps you nice and warm.

490
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:19,260
We have a fireplace for warmth.

491
00:31:19,261 --> 00:31:20,886
I mean, I'm ready to tailgate.

492
00:31:20,887 --> 00:31:24,300
It doesn't matter how cold it is, I'll be there for last night.

493
00:31:24,480 --> 00:31:30,140
It started out very small, and then we graduated to this, and we're getting a port-a-potty for the next year's tailgating bash.

494
00:31:30,480 --> 00:31:36,200
Look at this. Flowers on the walls and everything. There you go. This is tailgating.

495
00:31:37,200 --> 00:31:42,320
But the most impressive gear in the tailgating industry isn't what you wear or what you cook on.

496
00:31:42,680 --> 00:31:47,300
It's where you live while you're parked in the lot. It's the crib.

497
00:31:47,301 --> 00:31:51,560
You know what I'm talking about. Nearly every home team has one.

498
00:31:52,020 --> 00:31:55,780
In Minnesota, there's the Viking Battle Wagon, complete with a gas fireplace,

499
00:31:56,220 --> 00:31:58,920
paneled walls, and grandma's easy chair.

500
00:31:59,820 --> 00:32:04,680
New York Giants fans have been partying with the Pumpkin Gang van for over 47 years.

501
00:32:05,040 --> 00:32:08,240
You've got to have a bus. You don't have a bus, you don't even belong here.

502
00:32:08,280 --> 00:32:09,120
You're not even in the game.

503
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:11,540
They come in all shapes and sizes.

504
00:32:11,640 --> 00:32:16,280
This was a 1977 trail that we just redid, you know.

505
00:32:16,281 --> 00:32:20,080
and this is the original six guys that started with us right over here.

506
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:23,440
It's all dry in colors, dry in merrivelia in here.

507
00:32:23,620 --> 00:32:25,580
The most essential item now is the van.

508
00:32:26,220 --> 00:32:28,460
Next year we're going to upgrade, we're going to improve,

509
00:32:28,640 --> 00:32:30,760
we're going to do some more custom artwork to the van.

510
00:32:31,960 --> 00:32:34,760
Some spend a lot of money. Others...

511
00:32:34,761 --> 00:32:37,140
Boys, want to take a look at the car? Come on.

512
00:32:37,280 --> 00:32:38,900
This is actually a one-family car.

513
00:32:39,080 --> 00:32:42,220
89 Ford Taurus wagon. My sister bought it brand new in 89.

514
00:32:42,520 --> 00:32:44,920
She sold it to my mom, the transmission went out on it,

515
00:32:44,921 --> 00:32:48,880
And I bought her a minivan, put a transmission in it, and this is what we have.

516
00:32:49,240 --> 00:32:51,220
It's our tailgating vehicle, and I drive it every day.

517
00:32:52,040 --> 00:32:55,720
For those more invested in their ride, there's this pickup made by Galpin Motors.

518
00:32:55,721 --> 00:32:59,920
It comes with beer taps, TV, and a grill all in one.

519
00:33:00,460 --> 00:33:05,380
These days, if you really want to tailgate in style, not just any old van is going to do.

520
00:33:05,880 --> 00:33:08,000
You're going to need one of these guys.

521
00:33:08,680 --> 00:33:12,060
The tailgater's ultimate pipe dream, an RV.

522
00:33:12,900 --> 00:33:18,520
It's now common for people to spend hundreds of thousands of dollars on large recreational vehicles

523
00:33:18,521 --> 00:33:23,380
that they use to transport themselves and their friends and their family to the tailgate spot.

524
00:33:23,820 --> 00:33:28,580
And sometimes folks might not drive it more than 10, 15, 20 miles.

525
00:33:29,020 --> 00:33:34,280
They happen to live right near the stadium, and they've invested all this money just to tailgate.

526
00:33:34,281 --> 00:33:37,360
You certainly see enormous RVs coming into the stadium,

527
00:33:37,480 --> 00:33:40,760
and the RVs tend to come in on Thursday night for the Saturday night game.

528
00:33:42,060 --> 00:33:45,120
According to the Recreational Vehicle Industry Association,

529
00:33:45,500 --> 00:33:49,340
approximately 7.2 million people own RVs in the U.S.

530
00:33:49,940 --> 00:33:51,560
In the opinion of the tailgater,

531
00:33:51,920 --> 00:33:55,660
there's no better place to put them into action than the stadium lot.

532
00:33:56,580 --> 00:34:00,800
In Miami, Karen Kendall and the Hot Mamas drive their RV to every home game,

533
00:34:00,801 --> 00:34:05,560
where they host tailgating parties for over 200 students and do lots of shots.

534
00:34:06,140 --> 00:34:10,740
Welcome to our Miami Hurricanes tailgate RV.

535
00:34:10,741 --> 00:34:15,640
We have a 42-foot country coach, Millennium Series, year 2000.

536
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:20,180
Yes, keeping up with the Joneses is coming to a stadium near you.

537
00:34:20,920 --> 00:34:21,760
Love Jets.

538
00:34:22,180 --> 00:34:29,100
In Dallas, Cowboy fan Vince Vance drives his custom-designed RV to the Cowboys' Thanksgiving Day game.

539
00:34:29,460 --> 00:34:32,400
People in Oakland may show up in their Coronas and their Subarus.

540
00:34:32,920 --> 00:34:38,300
We show up in our $400,000 Silver Eagle bus.

541
00:34:38,580 --> 00:34:39,680
Please, let me show you.

542
00:34:39,681 --> 00:34:46,200
Now, if you move your camera up, you see the mirrors on the ceiling, the marble floors.

543
00:34:47,040 --> 00:34:53,160
In Lambeau Field, though, this is probably the supreme grip, belonging to none other than the Pacalope.

544
00:34:53,600 --> 00:34:55,520
It's not exactly an RV.

545
00:34:56,100 --> 00:34:58,700
The hood of the van, I added Super Bowls 1, 2, and 31.

546
00:34:59,920 --> 00:35:01,940
Those are the three Super Bowls the Packers won.

547
00:35:01,941 --> 00:35:09,360
Yes, sir, the big business of football has made it possible to have all the comforts of home

548
00:35:09,361 --> 00:35:13,640
in the barren expanse of an asphalt parking lot, including the house.

549
00:35:14,800 --> 00:35:18,780
It's made watching the game more enjoyable, the tailgate competition bolder,

550
00:35:18,980 --> 00:35:23,680
and the fans, well, quite honestly, it's made them a bit more insane.

551
00:35:26,580 --> 00:35:29,980
You've seen the over-the-top food, ogle the expensive gear.

552
00:35:29,981 --> 00:35:32,860
Now check out the out of control fans

553
00:35:32,861 --> 00:35:34,840
When we come back

554
00:35:34,841 --> 00:35:37,740
See what it takes to survive in the parking lot on game day

555
00:35:37,741 --> 00:35:39,660
It's tailgaters gone wild

556
00:35:39,661 --> 00:35:41,300
When tailgating returns

557
00:35:41,301 --> 00:35:43,560
The only home jet game I ever missed

558
00:35:43,561 --> 00:35:44,380
Was to get married

559
00:35:44,381 --> 00:35:45,840
And I shouldn't have missed that one

560
00:35:45,841 --> 00:35:50,300
If you're a tailgater

561
00:35:50,301 --> 00:35:53,380
You know the importance of arriving well before kickoff

562
00:35:53,381 --> 00:35:55,560
10% of tailgaters set up

563
00:35:55,561 --> 00:35:57,520
1-2 hours before a game

564
00:35:57,521 --> 00:36:00,700
51% set up three to four hours before

565
00:36:00,701 --> 00:36:05,980
And an amazing 38% set up more than five hours before game time

566
00:36:05,981 --> 00:36:08,700
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment

567
00:36:08,701 --> 00:36:13,300
Sport, the ultimate athletic contest of man

568
00:36:13,301 --> 00:36:16,060
Sporting events can bring out the best in us

569
00:36:16,061 --> 00:36:17,620
Inspire us

570
00:36:17,621 --> 00:36:19,160
Leave us in awe

571
00:36:19,161 --> 00:36:24,480
And turn us into screaming, raging maniacs

572
00:36:24,481 --> 00:36:26,940
These are the Fanatics

573
00:36:26,941 --> 00:36:31,740
the 4% of tailgaters who attend 16 or more football games a season.

574
00:36:32,020 --> 00:36:34,840
The only home jet game I ever missed was to get married,

575
00:36:34,960 --> 00:36:36,280
and I shouldn't have missed that one.

576
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:40,160
Guys like Dolphin Dan of Miami.

577
00:36:40,420 --> 00:36:44,200
For 20 years, this Miami standout shows up in his shoulder pads

578
00:36:44,201 --> 00:36:45,420
to support the home team.

579
00:36:45,600 --> 00:36:48,320
The only thing that changes is the jersey he wears,

580
00:36:48,540 --> 00:36:49,720
dolphin or hurricane.

581
00:36:50,260 --> 00:36:52,380
I'm looking for a sponsor to the Fiesta Bowl,

582
00:36:52,520 --> 00:36:53,800
somebody will help me out.

583
00:36:55,180 --> 00:36:56,180
Tailgate Russ.

584
00:36:56,760 --> 00:36:58,960
Starting with a strong rookie season in 1980,

585
00:36:59,400 --> 00:37:03,340
this tailgater out of Susquehanna University has put up some consistent numbers

586
00:37:03,341 --> 00:37:05,160
each and every tailgating season.

587
00:37:05,300 --> 00:37:08,400
Start out with 40 people in 98, 200 people in 99.

588
00:37:08,680 --> 00:37:12,300
But this grizzled tailgating veteran keeps his eye on the prize.

589
00:37:12,420 --> 00:37:13,940
Earlier this year, I had one for $750.

590
00:37:14,560 --> 00:37:17,960
And it goes a thousand-person tailgate, so I'm still working on that goal.

591
00:37:18,420 --> 00:37:19,480
The Pack-A-Lope.

592
00:37:19,780 --> 00:37:24,780
He's only missed five Green Bay Packers home games in a 28-year span.

593
00:37:24,781 --> 00:37:28,800
The caricatures of myself, the pack lope, and my wife, Mrs. Lope.

594
00:37:29,640 --> 00:37:33,700
But hands down, the tailgater with the most impressive record is Joe Kahn.

595
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:41,440
Over the last six tailgating seasons, Joe has traveled over 200,000 miles to over 90 football stadiums.

596
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:43,740
He calls himself the commissioner of tailgating.

597
00:37:44,120 --> 00:37:46,160
I am a diehard tailgater.

598
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:52,440
Even when there's no game, I'm going to pull up in somebody's parking lot and set out a grill.

599
00:37:52,441 --> 00:37:56,000
Not just any weekend warrior can be an extreme tailgater.

600
00:37:56,500 --> 00:37:59,860
It takes very special qualities to make it to the top 4%,

601
00:37:59,861 --> 00:38:02,240
traits that only a few among us possess.

602
00:38:03,340 --> 00:38:04,960
Number one, it takes heart.

603
00:38:05,780 --> 00:38:10,140
You have to be there for your team, weekend in, weekend out.

604
00:38:10,380 --> 00:38:12,520
When they suit up, you suit up.

605
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:17,940
I go to St. Vince to all the home games and as many away games as possible.

606
00:38:18,500 --> 00:38:20,980
They've got these crazy-ass outfits they put on,

607
00:38:20,981 --> 00:38:23,660
so they come out of the closet, so to speak.

608
00:38:23,900 --> 00:38:25,180
They're ready to compete.

609
00:38:25,580 --> 00:38:27,900
They're ready to identify the enemy,

610
00:38:28,300 --> 00:38:31,380
to be part of the action.

611
00:38:31,620 --> 00:38:33,720
When you dress up and paint your face

612
00:38:33,721 --> 00:38:35,620
and you're a banker,

613
00:38:36,320 --> 00:38:38,420
it's not something you do every day,

614
00:38:38,520 --> 00:38:39,880
but I'll tell you for the team,

615
00:38:39,980 --> 00:38:40,840
we're going to do anything.

616
00:38:41,220 --> 00:38:43,060
Get the growl towel going today.

617
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:45,420
The growl towel.

618
00:38:45,421 --> 00:38:46,121
Woo!

619
00:38:47,460 --> 00:38:49,700
But having heart is only part of it.

620
00:38:49,701 --> 00:38:54,800
To make it to the top four, you also need quality number two, resilience.

621
00:38:55,640 --> 00:38:57,620
Keep your toes and your hands warm.

622
00:38:57,880 --> 00:39:02,440
You have to be prepared to encounter the harshest weather conditions North America can throw.

623
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:04,920
Failure is not an option.

624
00:39:05,100 --> 00:39:07,840
I have about six or seven layers on the bottom.

625
00:39:08,220 --> 00:39:11,960
That's how they do it at Lambeau Field, even the girls.

626
00:39:12,180 --> 00:39:14,620
And, of course, big gloves.

627
00:39:14,621 --> 00:39:20,100
Occasionally up at Lambeau, you know, it could be a life-threatening situation because of the weather.

628
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:25,020
You know, but if you're a true fan, you know, you go there and you sit with it.

629
00:39:25,100 --> 00:39:27,300
And then if you've got to go to the hospital later, you go to the hospital.

630
00:39:27,640 --> 00:39:28,883
It's not too cold today.

631
00:39:28,884 --> 00:39:32,300
It's only about, windchill's about zero what I heard this morning.

632
00:39:32,860 --> 00:39:36,160
Green Bay has got a long history of weather-immune fans.

633
00:39:37,100 --> 00:39:41,640
Lambeau has the distinction of holding the record for the coldest NFL football game in history.

634
00:39:41,641 --> 00:39:46,960
The Packer-Cowboy matchup of 1967, better known as the Ice Bowl.

635
00:39:47,580 --> 00:39:50,040
The temperature, minus 13 degrees.

636
00:39:50,660 --> 00:39:55,320
The fans, out in full force to watch the Packers plow the Cowboys.

637
00:39:56,340 --> 00:40:00,420
I have a special piece of clothing that keeps me warm and cheesy.

638
00:40:01,280 --> 00:40:02,660
There it is.

639
00:40:03,260 --> 00:40:05,460
So, you think you've got heart and resilience?

640
00:40:05,820 --> 00:40:07,280
How about quality number three?

641
00:40:08,080 --> 00:40:08,780
Durability.

642
00:40:09,160 --> 00:40:11,820
You've got to be ready to take a hit like a quarterback.

643
00:40:12,660 --> 00:40:13,680
Take it to the turf.

644
00:40:14,240 --> 00:40:15,600
Defend your territory.

645
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:18,360
We pull in a little bit before six.

646
00:40:19,300 --> 00:40:25,900
I see two grown men, 60-plus, about getting into a fight about who's parked and who's spot.

647
00:40:25,960 --> 00:40:30,040
The thing about football is since it is a, you know, a quote-unquote violent sport,

648
00:40:30,520 --> 00:40:32,500
the tailgating is a little more violent.

649
00:40:32,640 --> 00:40:35,020
You see more tackling, knocking each other down.

650
00:40:35,440 --> 00:40:38,400
You want to have the feeling that those guys get out there when they hit each other.

651
00:40:38,401 --> 00:40:39,260
and men love that.

652
00:40:39,480 --> 00:40:41,520
That's how the top four percenters do it

653
00:40:41,521 --> 00:40:44,620
in every stadium across this great land of ours.

654
00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:46,540
And it's not just the guys.

655
00:40:47,680 --> 00:40:49,380
Got pushed by a woman

656
00:40:49,381 --> 00:40:51,160
and wanted to start a fight with me.

657
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:55,300
And last but not least,

658
00:40:55,540 --> 00:40:57,880
for when number three goes too far

659
00:40:57,881 --> 00:40:59,960
to make it as an extreme tailgater,

660
00:41:00,180 --> 00:41:02,240
you're going to need a number four,

661
00:41:02,840 --> 00:41:03,540
a lawyer.

662
00:41:04,340 --> 00:41:07,120
Especially if you're in the city of brotherly love.

663
00:41:07,121 --> 00:41:13,760
Philadelphia fans have earned the undisputed reputation of being some of the most fiery fans in the land.

664
00:41:13,761 --> 00:41:15,900
They kick fights, they throw food.

665
00:41:16,100 --> 00:41:16,960
Go history!

666
00:41:17,740 --> 00:41:19,820
They kick beer cans at you.

667
00:41:19,920 --> 00:41:26,660
Philadelphia fans are very crazy because they're also sort of mean-spirited, and that's good.

668
00:41:26,760 --> 00:41:31,260
You go along the cars and they ride eagles on your car when they know that you're from Giants Stadium.

669
00:41:31,620 --> 00:41:33,240
Just nasty, nasty.

670
00:41:33,241 --> 00:41:43,740
Loud and boisterous tailgating is definitely a catalyst to loud and obnoxious fans in the stadium.

671
00:41:44,400 --> 00:41:50,380
The way that they prepare, the way that they get fueled up before the contest at their tailgates

672
00:41:50,381 --> 00:41:55,180
definitely plays a large part into the crowd atmosphere once you get into the stadium.

673
00:41:55,420 --> 00:41:56,740
There's nothing like the Philadelphia Passion.

674
00:41:57,180 --> 00:41:58,240
Nothing like Philadelphia Passion.

675
00:41:58,320 --> 00:41:59,320
You can't find it anywhere.

676
00:42:00,020 --> 00:42:01,620
You sure can't.

677
00:42:01,621 --> 00:42:07,900
On one occasion in 1968, Santa Claus visited halftime one game to cheer on the crowd.

678
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:15,140
The fan 99 fans jeered Dallas Cowboys receiver Michael Irvin as he lay on the field for 20 minutes,

679
00:42:15,460 --> 00:42:17,860
suffering from a neck injury that ended his career.

680
00:42:18,800 --> 00:42:22,180
The rowdy fan behavior had clearly gotten out of control.

681
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:27,020
Enter Judge Seamus McCaffrey, the vet's answer to Judge Wapner.

682
00:42:27,460 --> 00:42:29,860
For rowdy tailgaters, he's the man.

683
00:42:29,861 --> 00:42:37,640
If you break the law on his beat, you can get booked, locked up, and sentenced all in the same amount of time as it takes to watch the halftime show.

684
00:42:38,000 --> 00:42:45,986
After these incidents and other rowdy behavior in the parking lot, the Philadelphia Police Department, 4th District Headquarters, and Municipal Court moved into a

685
00:42:45,987 --> 00:42:47,120
locker room at the vet.

686
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:53,460
Despite the crackdown and the move to Lincoln Financial Field, things haven't exactly mellowed out.

687
00:42:54,100 --> 00:42:58,280
The Link is outfitted with 100 digital security cameras throughout the stadium

688
00:42:58,281 --> 00:43:01,260
and four very large jail cells.

689
00:43:02,000 --> 00:43:04,160
Oh, and the judge is there, too.

690
00:43:06,060 --> 00:43:08,000
But why dwell upon the negative?

691
00:43:08,480 --> 00:43:10,780
When you strip tailgating down to its fundamentals,

692
00:43:10,781 --> 00:43:15,360
when you take away the food, take away the competition, take away the gear,

693
00:43:15,660 --> 00:43:20,620
there's only one thing left, and it's the most important thing of all, the people.

694
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:24,340
And really, they're what tailgating is all about.

695
00:43:25,200 --> 00:43:27,540
We used to sit out on our stoops.

696
00:43:28,760 --> 00:43:32,680
We used to sit out on our porches and wave to our neighbors as they walked by.

697
00:43:32,681 --> 00:43:34,920
In America, we've lost this.

698
00:43:35,620 --> 00:43:39,700
What we see in the parking lot is people talking to each other.

699
00:43:40,320 --> 00:43:42,560
They're not sitting in front of a computer.

700
00:43:43,220 --> 00:43:46,320
They're not watching television to keep company.

701
00:43:46,720 --> 00:43:49,680
Everyone gets together for a common cause.

702
00:43:49,681 --> 00:43:52,680
And isn't that really what a community is about?

703
00:43:52,681 --> 00:43:55,680
Saturday nights, you can't wait for the night to get over.

704
00:43:55,681 --> 00:44:01,680
You go to bed as early as you can so you can get up early and get up here and start tailgating.

705
00:44:01,681 --> 00:44:04,680
The greatest thing ever happened. Tailgating is really nice.

706
00:44:04,681 --> 00:44:07,680
I have a lot of good friends and you can't beat them.

